1:# Round 1 synthesis — the carried-forward architecture
2:
3:*Orchestrator, 2026-09-30. Inputs: five concepts (C1 Market, C2 Co-founder, C3 Swarm [Codex], C4 Lab, C5 Studio) and two
4:independent judges (J1 Claude, J2 Codex). This is the frame Round 2 designs inside. Seats may challenge it — upward only.*
5:
6:## 1. What the judges said
7:
8:| Concept | J1 Claude | J2 Codex | Mean | Strongest mechanism (both judges agree) |
9:|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
10:| C1 Market | 43 | 44 | 43.5 | Framing Contract / independently settled Outcome Contracts |
11:| C2 Co-founder | 45 | 48 | 46.5 | Judgment compiled into Standing Orders; founder-model vs own-view split |
12:| C3 Swarm | 43 | **52** | 47.5 | Fenced leases + effect gateway + receipts — the most robust substrate |
13:| C4 Lab | **48** | 49 | **48.5** | Preregistered bets, default-kill, learning as the architecture |
14:| C5 Studio | 46 | 47 | 46.5 | Backlot with mandatory strike; Dailies Reel + circled takes |
15:
16:**They disagreed on the spine** (J1: Lab; J2: Swarm) **and agreed on every graft.** The disagreement dissolves once you see
17:that each judge picked a different *layer*: J2 picked the execution substrate, J1 the learning-and-allocation discipline.
18:Both are needed and neither is the whole. The carried-forward design is therefore not "one winner with grafts" but
19:**four layers with separated authority**, J2's framing, filled with J1's grafts.
20:
21:## 2. The organising principle
22:
23:> **Separate the four authorities — Intent, Allocation, Execution, Acceptance — so that no agent both decides what matters,
24:> funds it, does it and judges it. Everything learned flows back as tested policy, reusable assets and calibrated priors.**
25:
26:Working name for the whole: **the Compounding Organisation** (the practice it enables: vibe startuping).
27:
28:```mermaid
29:flowchart TB
30:  F[Founder — intent, taste, one-way doors] --> I
31:  subgraph I[INTENT — C2]
32:    VM[Venture Mind per venture<br/>charter · theses · Standing Orders · wagers]
33:    PM[Portfolio Mind]
34:  end
35:  subgraph A[ALLOCATION — C4 + C1]
36:    AL[Allocator: investment lane<br/>bets · VoI · calibrated forecasts]
37:    AF[Attention field: obligations lane<br/>incidents · customer promises]
38:    FX[Founder Attention Exchange]
39:  end
40:  subgraph E[EXECUTION — C3 substrate, shape chosen per mission]
41:    M[Missions → teams by title+expertise<br/>Claude Code & Codex equal]
42:    WM[(World model / company brain)]
43:    L[Leases · merge queues · receipts]
44:    G[Effect gateway → outside world]
45:  end
46:  subgraph X[ACCEPTANCE — C4 Referee]
47:    R[Cross-family Referee outside the worker sandbox<br/>reads systems of record]
48:  end
49:  I --> A --> E --> X
50:  X -- receipts, outcomes --> LRN
51:  subgraph LRN[LEARNING — compounding stores]
52:    PL[Priors Library + Null Registry]
53:    BL[Backlot: reusable assets, mandatory strike]
54:    SO[Standing Orders + skills, promoted by trial]
55:    CAL[Calibration ledger: agents, configs, founder]
56:  end
57:  LRN --> I
58:  LRN --> A
59:  X -. dailies · circled takes .-> F
60:```
61:
62:## 3. The vocabulary (use these words; define new ones only if needed)
63:
64:| Term | Source | Meaning |
65:|---|---|---|
66:| **Venture** | — | Any project: startup, agency, business, research, learning. Has a **Charter**: intent, autonomy level, budget, never-list, data boundary. |
67:| **Venture Mind** | C2 | Persistent, versioned record of a venture's theses, taste, Standing Orders, wagers and commitments. The **Co-founder seat** is any Claude or Codex session that loads it — a role, not a process. A **Portfolio Mind** spans ventures. |
68:| **Standing Order** | C2 | A decision policy (never a method) with `valid_until`, compiled from repeated founder/Mind decisions. KPI: share of decisions resolved by policy. |
69:| **Mission** | C3/C4 | The unit of funded work. Its **shape is chosen per mission** — solo, lead+workers, swarm, audition/tournament — because R0-A shows no shape wins everywhere. |
70:| **Bet** | C4 | A preregistered hypothesis with success and kill criteria and a kill date. Required only when a result enters the Priors Library, buys a tranche, or crosses a door type. Routine two-way work runs unregistered and is logged as observation. |
71:| **Framing Contract** | C1 | When no measure of success exists, the first mission buys one. |
72:| **Evidence ladder × door type** | C4 | Evidence required scales with irreversibility; sets both autonomy limits and founder altitude. |
73:| **Two lanes** | C3+C4 | **Obligations lane** (incidents, customer commitments) ordered by an attention field, never waits for a bet cycle. **Investment lane** funded by the Allocator. |
74:| **World model** | C3 | Evidence-linked per-venture company brain: customers, market, competitors, metrics, decisions. |
75:| **Lease / receipt / effect gateway** | C3 | Fenced leases stop stale workers; every outside-world effect passes one gateway (idempotent, budget- and policy-checked) and leaves a receipt. |
76:| **Referee** | C4 | Acceptance by the *other* model family from the builder, outside the worker's sandbox, reading systems of record (Stripe, CI, analytics) — never the worker's claims. |
77:| **Founder Attention Exchange** | C1 | Decisions compete for a fixed daily supply of founder minutes; two-way doors proceed on silence. |
78:| **Dailies Reel / circled takes** | C5 | Raw artifacts shown to the founder; what he circles becomes taste data. |
79:| **Backlot** | C5 | Versioned reusable assets (code, brand kits, audiences, infra). A mission must strike improvements back on wrap. |
80:| **Series mode** | C5 | Ongoing autonomous operation of a venture that never "ends". |
81:| **Ledger** | C1/C4 | One ledger for cost, forecasts, wagers and calibration. **No stakes and no transferable rewards** — they reward gaming the Referee (R0-A: evaluator hacking). |
82:| **Priors Library / Null Registry** | C4 | What the organisation believes, with evidence level; null results kept, not buried. Memory that no settled work cites decays (C1 royalties ⇄ read-and-use tracking). |
83:
84:## 4. Resolved conflicts (binding for Round 2 unless challenged upward)
85:1. **No central boss, but not headless.** The Mind owns thesis and proposes allocation; it never holds verdict or merge
86:   authority. The Referee cannot be overruled by the Mind; the founder can overrule anything.
87:2. **One allocator, two lanes.** Obligations first (reserved capacity), then investment by the Allocator. Auditions
88:   (C1) choose approaches *inside* funded missions. Capacity is never spent twice.
89:3. **Departments are stores and policies, not standing agents.** The only persistent things are records: Minds, backlot,
90:   cast registry, ledger, world model. Every agent is launched on demand and dissolved.
91:4. **Kill dates stop hypotheses, never obligations.** Customer promises survive a killed bet via wind-down missions.
92:5. **Continuity without freezing mistakes.** The Fingerprint gate (C2) preserves *constraints*; outcomes may overturn past
93:   choices. It generalises to every config promotion — models, prompts, skills, identities.
94:6. **Standing Orders are policies.** One that dictates steps fails lint — the same rule the harness applies to playbooks.
95:7. **No span-of-control caps borrowed from humans** (R0-B). Fan-out is limited by verifier capacity (R0-A: MAST, 17.2× vs
96:   4.4× error amplification), not by a number of reports.
97:8. **Correlated failure is budgeted.** One defective skill or model deployed everywhere is a portfolio risk (J2).
98:
99:## 5. What all five concepts missed — now required
100:Round 2 seats must pick these up (owner in brackets):
101:- **Legal-financial body** — entities, contracts, tax, books, banking, liability for autonomous actions [startup operator, external world].
102:- **Founder continuity** — founder state, dead-man switch, succession [autonomy].
103:- **Counterparty agents** — customers' and suppliers' agents; A2A commerce; inbound injection defence [multi-agent, engineering].
104:- **Reputation as a breakable asset** — AI disclosure, per-brand firewall, outbound claims standard, global kill [external world].
105:- **Model-release reflex** — re-benchmark every config; ask what the new capability makes possible [evals, skills].
106:- **Human task market** — humans hired for signatures, physical work, human-only calls, on the same ledger [external world].
107:- **Self-funding capital loops and exits** — revenue recycled to compute by treasury rule; sell / spin out / shut as designed outcomes [economics, operator].
108:- **A measure of "out-building thousands"** — e.g. FTE-equivalent output per founder-hour on matched deliverables [evals, vision].
109:- **Inter-venture economy** — ventures as each other's first customers [operator].
110:- **Missing-evidence audits, complementary-investment bundles, relationship repair, transferable operating-company packages** (J2) [lab/evals, allocator, operator].
111:
112:## 6. What is still open for Round 2 to settle
113:- Autonomy levels: the concepts proposed A0–A4 (C2), A0–A3 + Series (C5), mandates (C1). **Autonomy seat decides.**
114:- Founder-contact classes: ENGINE-SPEC and SURFACES-SPEC disagree. **Surfaces seat and autonomy seat reconcile jointly** — surfaces owns the rendering, autonomy owns the classes.
115:- Runtime language, log store, sandbox: open. **Engineering seat decides**, starting from ENGINE-SPEC.
116:- Whether the Allocator uses Thompson sampling, VoI ranking, or both. **Mission-engine seat decides.**

