docs/vision-system/planning/specification/07-integrations-capacity.md:174:**SOURCE CLAIM — FAL-01, carried into this comparator so a capacity reading need not leave the specification to find it (F6C-18).** [The F2 selection record](../F2/05-selection-record.md) §8.3 falsifies a strength the frozen protocol granted **B0**, the single long-context-session baseline: **B0 does not have the lowest plausible consumption of scarce capacity.** Four documented provider mechanisms contradict it — the full conversation is sent with every request and again with every tool batch; the first message after a break longer than the cache lifetime reprocesses the full context; compaction reads the conversation it summarises and is itself a large request; and idle check-ins each send the full context. **B0 minimises launches; it does not minimise the weekly bucket, and the weekly bucket binds** [R8 B-6]. **What this does and does not do to the rows above.** The tables in this section price **money only**, claim no capacity advantage for S1 in either direction, and close with *allow S0 to win* — all of which stands. What the falsifier removes is the **intuitive** capacity argument, that the option making the fewest launches consumes the least scarce capacity; that is the reading a reader comparing S0 or any simpler option on capacity would otherwise reach here unaided, and it is falsified by a primary source this round already holds. **UNKNOWN:** the weekly-bucket consumption of S1, S1-VPS and S0 is unmeasured; §6's expected-versus-actual native-launch count (R-X12) is the owed instrument, and until it runs no row in this section carries a capacity comparison at all.
