docs/vision-v2/README.md:3:**Summary.** v2 is a small operating system for one owner running several ventures and projects from one Mac on two subscriptions: Claude Code builds and Codex reviews. It keeps this repo's harness (7 engines, QA gate, claim ledger, lenses, playbooks, skills, sandbox) and adds one missing layer: a **runner**. The runner is a host process with a SQLite queue that creates worktrees, launches engines by argv, and **decides each job's status itself**: a diff exists, a frozen done-test passes in a clean checkout, and the harness loaded. The founder's view is a Monday page, a capped daily inbox and push alerts only for real emergencies. Memory is markdown in git plus the claim ledger and SQLite search. Each venture is its own repo and cannot read another's. Legal, finance, support and self-improvement stay dormant until named triggers. The build is ~30 small jobs, starting with a thin end-to-end path, and ends with one integrated test seeded with 11 failure classes. **The system is accepted when a real venture reaches Problem-validated through it.**
docs/vision-system/planning/site/explorer/data/pins.json:1:{"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json","schema_version":"1.0","kind":"pinned conjuncts: hand-written assertions about the LIVE registries","items":[{"id":"schema_version","why":null,"value":"1.0","source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/schema_version"},{"id":"kind","why":null,"value":"pinned conjuncts: hand-written assertions about the LIVE registries","source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/kind"},{"id":"why","why":null,"value":"RC-02. The criterion-content oracle compares predicate-registry.json to tools/phase_content.py and fails on any difference -- which makes a HAND edit of a criterion a failing build, and makes a DERIVATION edit invisible. The recheck measured it: one hunk replacing the ('SalesAgreement','performed') spec, then `python3 tools/author_phase_content.py`, and the whole G2-01 defect is back at exit 0, 268,829 checks, with all 27 negative fixtures still rejecting. A checker that compares an artifact to a source the same author controls is not a checker. Every assertion below is written BY HAND against the live registry and is never generated from anything: author_phase_content.py does not read this file and cannot write it, so regenerating the registry from a weakened derivation now fails HERE, naming the conjunct that went missing. RC2-02, the same argument one operator over and found the same way -- by mutation, not by reading: these rows asserted CONTAINMENT, and a conjunct kept verbatim inside an `any` beside a trivially-true alternative is contained by the body while being demanded by nothing. Every `why` below is written as a demand, so each row now says which it means: `disjoined` defaults to false and the four rows whose requirement really is conditional name their branch.","source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/why"},{"id":"how_to_read","why":null,"value":{"pin":"one predicate id, the findings and decisions that put each requirement there, and the `require` rows that must hold of its body in the committed predicate-registry.json","matching":"a row matches when SOME node of the body carries that `op` and satisfies every constraint on the row. Structural containment only: a body may carry more than is pinned, never less.","negated":"false (the default) requires a matching node that is NOT beneath a `not`; true requires one that IS. Without this, wrapping a pinned conjunct in `not` would satisfy a containment check while inverting the requirement.","disjoined":"false (the default) requires a matching node that is NOT beneath an `any`: the body must DEMAND the conjunct, not merely mention it in one branch of a disjunction. true permits a match inside one, for a requirement that is genuinely conditional, and then `disjoined_why` must name the condition. RC2-02 is why it exists and it was measured, not argued: disjoining each of the three pinned conjuncts of criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1 with `present(/payload/agreed_terms)` -- a field records.schema.json already requires of every SalesAgreement -- left every pin matching, this file untouched, and validate_contracts.py at exit 0 with 31 of 31 negative fixtures still rejecting, over a criterion that demanded none of it. Four rows opt in; each names its branch. Disjunction is read through negation rather than beside it, because `not(any(A, B))` demands `not A` while `not(all(A, B))` demands neither.","disjoined_why":"required on a row with `disjoined: true` and REFUSED on any other row. An opt-out of the demand rule states which condition makes the requirement conditional, in the same way `unpinnable` states why no conjunct can carry a finding: an excuse nobody had to write is an excuse nobody reads.","states_exactly":"the binding's `states` list must equal this set. A superset is a weakening -- `['delivered','proposed']` admits the exact counterexample AD-013 was decided to refuse -- and a subset is a different requirement than the one recorded.","closed_key_set":"validate_contracts.py declares the constraint keys it reads and REFUSES any other key in a row. A pin whose constraint the checker silently ignored would be the same defect one layer up: a control that reads as enforcement while nothing checks it."},"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/how_to_read"},{"id":"checked_by","why":null,"value":["validate_contracts.py: each pin against predicate-registry.json directly, with no import of tools/phase_content.py","validate_contracts.py: coverage -- every finding id swept out of registers/review-findings.json is pinned here or declared in `unpinnable` with a reason","fixtures/negative/r13-derivation-weakened-then-regenerated: patches tools/phase_content.py, regenerates with author_phase_content.py, and must be REJECTED by these pins","fixtures/negative/r14-pinned-conjunct-disjoined-with-a-tautology: disjoins each pinned conjunct of criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1 with a tautology, regenerates with author_phase_content.py, and must be REJECTED naming the disjunction -- r13-derivation-weakened-then-regenerated cannot catch this, because it REMOVES the conjuncts rather than weakening them"],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/checked_by"},{"id":"admissible_ancestors","why":"RC4-02, and it is the structural rule this whole block now turns on: A REGISTRY MAY CLASSIFY POSITIONS; ONLY THIS FILE MAY SAY WHAT SATISFIES ONE. RC3-01's repair moved the definition of \"demanded\" out of a hard-coded triple in validate_contracts.py and into primitive-registry.json's `argument_positions` -- which the derivation's author also writes. The fourth recheck measured what that bought: register a NEW primitive `every_linked_obligation` with `argument_types.predicate: \"boolean\"` and `argument_positions.predicate: \"demanded\"`, wrap the three pinned conjuncts of criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1 in it, regenerate -- and the tree passes at exit 0 with 288,665 checks and 36 of 36 negative fixtures still rejecting, over a registry in which those conjuncts sit inside a quantifier over `/payload/obligation_refs`, a field records.schema.json gives no `minItems`. G2-01's counterexample, restored by a primitive that declared itself demanding. The table was closed against the UNDECLARED and open to the MISDECLARED. This table closes the second half: a pinned conjunct counts as demanded only when EVERY operator it sits inside, through a slot the registry calls a predicate position, is named here -- whatever `argument_positions` says about that operator. A primitive that declares its own child demanded and is not named here refuses the match, and the message names the operator.","value":{"all":{"registry_role":"demanded","admits":"always","why":"The conjunction. A child of `all` is true exactly when the body is, so a pinned conjunct sitting inside one is demanded with no condition. Measured over the committed corpus: every chain of demand-bearing ancestors of a matched pin row is drawn from `all`, `not` and `any` and from nothing else -- 3,318 of the RC-03 walk's positions sit under `('all',)` alone -- so this table costs no live match today and refuses every operator a future author could add."},"not":{"registry_role":"negated","admits":"row_polarity","why":"Negation is admissible because its whole effect on demand is POLARITY, and the row already states its polarity: `negated: true` requires a match beneath an odd number of `not`s and the default requires one beneath an even number. The containment filter in validate_contracts.py compares the two before this rule runs, so this entry is a backstop over that filter rather than the live control -- stated plainly, because a declaration whose condition nothing evaluates is the RC4 class one layer up. For a pinned VALUE op polarity is not a question at all (`path` and `resolve` return a JsonValue), and one live row matches a `path` reached through `eq.left` beneath `('all','any','not')`; `row_polarity` admits it for that reason and not by accident."},"any":{"registry_role":"disjoined","admits":"row_disjoined","why":"RC2-02's operator, and the only CONDITIONAL entry here. A conjunct kept verbatim in one branch of an `any` is contained by the body and demanded by nothing, so an `any` ancestor is admissible only for a row that opted in with `disjoined: true` and named its branch in `disjoined_why`. Four rows do, and the ceiling of four in validate_contracts.py is what stops a fifth arriving quietly. Read `admits` honestly: this condition and `not`'s are BACKSTOPS. The ancestor rule runs over the entries that would already satisfy the row, which the disjunction and polarity filters shaped first, so neither condition can fail on a tree those filters passed. MEMBERSHIP in this table is the live control; the conditions are written and evaluated so that the table states its reasons in something a checker reads rather than in prose."}},"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/admissible_ancestors"},{"id":"admissible_ancestors_not_admitted","why":null,"value":{"forall":"The registry's fourth boolean-child primitive, and RC3-01's route. `quantified` is unconditionally non-demanding here: a `forall` over a collection nothing forces non-empty is vacuously true, and nothing in this corpus forces any collection non-empty. Deliberately NOT given a conditional entry -- see RC4-04 and the note the quantifier refusal prints. Demanding the collection non-empty on a sibling conjunct does NOT lift the rule.","anything else":"Any primitive not named in `admissible_ancestors` -- including one registered tomorrow that declares its own `boolean` child `demanded`. That is RC4-02, and it is the reason this table is a closed set of NAMES rather than a rule over roles."},"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/admissible_ancestors_not_admitted"},{"id":"argument_positions_digest","why":"RC4-01. `primitive-registry.json` is written by tools/author_phase_content.py, tools/repair_r2_bootstrap_root.py and tools/repair_r3_intrinsic_subjects.py, and before this line nothing asserted a byte of it: the criterion-content drift oracle never compares the file, the guard ratchet only budgets sibling collisions, and there was no hash anywhere in the package. The fourth recheck flipped ONE string -- `/forall/argument_positions/predicate` from `\"quantified\"` to `\"demanded\"` -- and the vacuous-`forall` body validated at 288,646 checks with every pin satisfied, the census still reporting `under_quantifier: 0` because the census reads the same table. The only thing that refused it was a negative fixture naming `forall` by hand, and the sentence it printed named the FIXTURE rather than the table. This digest is sha256 over `{name: argument_positions}` for every primitive, sorted keys, compact separators, and it is MAINTAINED BY HAND: any edit to any argument position of any primitive fails the build until someone writes the new digest here, which is a line in a diff a reviewer sees. Recompute it with: python3 -c \"import json,hashlib; p=json.load(open('primitive-registry.json')); print(hashlib.sha256(json.dumps({k:v['argument_positions'] for k,v in sorted(p.items())},sort_keys=True,separators=(',',':')).encode()).hexdigest())\"","value":"c81e4dcb527a7137a61fa832490ad1cf9001b2d3ed4efa720f4e3cf12b59409e","source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/argument_positions_digest"},{"id":"finding_sources","why":null,"value":{"AC-M1-05":"planning/F2/reviews/A-company-human.md","AC-M3-02":"planning/F2/reviews/A-company-human.md","AC-M4-02":"planning/F2/reviews/A-company-human.md","AC-M4-05":"planning/F2/reviews/A-company-human.md","AE-M2-06":"planning/F2/reviews/A-economic-capacity.md","AE-M3-01":"planning/F2/reviews/A-economic-capacity.md","AE-M4-01":"planning/F2/reviews/A-economic-capacity.md","AE-M4-03":"planning/F2/reviews/A-econo