/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04a.md","F6Y-06":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04a.md","F6Y-07":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04a.md","F6Y-08":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04a.md","F6Z-01":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-02":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-03":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-04":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-05":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-06":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-07":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-08":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6Z-09":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-04b.md","F6AA-01":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-02":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-03":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-04":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-05":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-06":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-07":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-08":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-09":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-10":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md","F6AA-11":"planning/reviews/F2-06-recheck-05.md"},"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/finding_sources"},{"id":"step6_mention_only","why":"The Step 6 sweep in validate_contracts.py demands a `finding_sources` row for every id a review RAISES -- a heading or a table row. An id a review only MENTIONS, quoted from a fixture or cited from another document, is not a finding and holds no row. The difference between the two sets is declared here, by name and with a reason, and the validator checks that this map is EXACTLY that difference: so a review that quotes a new id is a data edit here, and a predicate that stops recognising raisings fails there rather than quietly narrowing the required set.","value":{"F6X-03":"A SYNTHETIC id. It exists only inside fixtures/positive/r36-a-declared-family-gains-a-finding-benign.json, whose whole purpose is to add a finding to a declared family and pass; F2-06-recheck-05.md describes that fixture and so quotes the id. No review raises it and no row is owed for it. Giving it a row instead would be answered by the next review that quotes any id."},"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/step6_mention_only"},{"id":"unpinnable","why":null,"value":[{"finding":"G1-06b","why":"A coverage finding about what the specification chapters SAY, closed by prose in the residue lane. No predicate expresses it, so a conjunct pin would be a pin on something else wearing its id."},{"finding":"RC-02","why":"This file IS the repair for RC-02. Pinning a conjunct 'for RC-02' would be circular; what enforces it is the coverage check below plus fixtures/negative/r13-derivation-weakened-then-regenerated."},{"finding":"RC-03","why":"A check on the relation between a criterion's `requires` sentence and its conjuncts, which is a property of every criterion rather than of any one predicate. Implemented in validate_contracts.py as the requires/conjuncts agreement check."},{"finding":"RC-04","why":"A property of the negative-fixture suite, not of the registry. Implemented as fixtures/negative/MANIFEST.json and the count ratchet in tools/run_negative_fixtures.py."},{"finding":"RC-05","why":"A property of how the runner resolves its own root. Implemented as the symlink refusal in tools/run_negative_fixtures.py."},{"finding":"RC-07","why":"A prose gap: `terminated_with_residuals` appears in no specification chapter. The phase-meaning row in chapter 03 closes it; no conjunct can."},{"finding":"XSR-R-01","why":"The subject is the cross-scope residue lane (docs/vision-xsr-residue): bindings, answers and verification_family_refs, none of which this package registers. No conjunct here can carry a statement about a document tree these contracts do not describe. Registered so the coverage check records the boundary rather than passing over it in silence."},{"finding":"XSR-R-02","why":"Same boundary as XSR-R-01: per-subcase positive controls and execution boundaries live in the cross-scope residue lane's verification tables. Nothing in record-registry.json or predicate-registry.json holds a subcase, so there is no predicate body a pin could name."},{"finding":"XSR-R-03","why":"Same boundary as XSR-R-01. `selected_route_ids` and `candidate_route_ids` are fields of the residue lane's route profiles, not of any record this package registers."},{"finding":"XSR-R-04","why":"Same boundary as XSR-R-01, and STILL OPEN by the register's own status: seven assertions are bound by no answer. A pin cannot carry it for the same reason -- the assertions are in the residue lane, not in this registry."},{"finding":"GR-01","why":"prose contract: the required contract is nine specification sentences kept verbatim under a supersession note. That is chapter prose, and a conjunct pin states what a predicate body must demand. What is mechanical about GR-01 -- that the note cites a predicate id that exists -- is already carried by the citation checks over the chapters."},{"finding":"GR-02","why":"OPEN AND ONLY PARTLY IMPLEMENTED, which is why no pin carries it: of the seven parked records the finding names, `Mission`, `Project`, `WorkflowRun` and `Case` now hold `parking_closure_account_ref` and `Goal`, `KnowledgeQuestion` and `Opportunity` do not. A pin over the four that have it would cite GR-02 and cover four sevenths of it, which reads as coverage of the whole. The finding is answered by pinning the parked criteria of all seven, or by the register recording why the other three need no closure account -- neither has happened. Deliberately NOT excused as prose: the contract names records this package registers."},{"finding":"GR-03","why":"Open by design, and it is the one finding whose required contract this package cannot hold at all: a predicate EVALUATOR over the 57 primitives, executing instance fixtures. Every check here is offline and structural -- no interpreter exists, and the verdict's own `limits` string says so. A pin asserts what a body demands; GR-03 asks for something that RUNS a body."},{"finding":"GR-04","why":"Implemented as a DERIVATION, not as a conjunct: values.schema.json's PredicateId enum is recomputed from predicate-registry.json and drift fails, and fixtures/negative/r12-predicate-id-enum-drifts-from-the-registry.json is the control. A pin over a schema enum would restate the derivation in the file the derivation writes."},{"finding":"RP-01","why":"Open, and a property of values.schema.json's TypedPredicate branches against each predicate's argument_schema -- a schema-to-schema comparison, not a demand any criterion body makes. The repair named in the contract is a derivation plus a negative fixture, in the shape GR-04 already took; neither is a pin."},{"finding":"RC2-01","why":"A property of how the runner resolves the fixture directories, closed by the symlink refusal in tools/run_negative_fixtures.py and tools/run_positive_fixtures.py. Same class as RC-05, which is declared unpinnable here for the same reason: no registry content is involved."},{"finding":"RC2-02","why":"The repair is the `disjoined` key on every `require` row, the `any`-ancestry tracking in the walker and the ceiling of four opted-in rows -- all of them properties of THIS FILE and of validate_contracts.py, not of any criterion body. fixtures/negative/r14-pinned-conjunct-disjoined-with-a-tautology is the control. A pin citing RC2-02 would be a pin about the shape of pins."},{"finding":"RC2-03","why":"A statement about the SCOPE of the RC-03 requires-rule, recorded in `#/requires_waivers_why` with its measured population. It is a limit this file publishes about a check, not a requirement a predicate body can demand."},{"finding":"RC2-04","why":"A branch in validate_contracts.py on the runner's payload, so a fixture count mismatch reports under the ratchet headline instead of the leak headline. A property of two checkers agreeing about a message; no conjunct can carry it."},{"finding":"RC3-01","why":"Closed by making the demand table derived: primitive-registry.json declares which child slots are demanding and the walker refuses any slot it does not recognise. The controls are fixtures/negative/r15-pinned-conjunct-inside-a-vacuous-forall and r15-unregistered-operator-in-a-predicate-position. The repair is the walker itself, so pinning a conjunct 'for RC3-01' would be circular in the way RC-02's row describes."},{"finding":"RC3-02","why":"Same root cause and same answer as RC3-01: the walker descends only into declared predicate positions and never into value slots. fixtures/negative/r15-pinned-conjunct-buried-in-a-value-slot is the control. A property of the walk, not of a body."},{"finding":"RC3-03","why":"A property of how the leak wrapper attaches the runner's structured payload instead of a stdout tail. Implemented in validate_contracts.py and tools/run_negative_fixtures.py; nothing in any registry expresses it."},{"finding":"RC4-01","why":"Implemented as `#/argument_positions_digest` in this file, compared against a hash of primitive-registry.json's argument_positions on every run, with fixtures/negative/r17-registry-flips-forall-to-demanded.json as the control. The digest IS the answer; a conjunct cannot carry a hash of the table that decides what a conjunct means."},{"finding":"RC4-02","why":"Implemented as the admissible-ancestor table -- now the closed literal ADMISSIBLE_LITERAL in validate_contracts.py with `#/admissible_ancestors` as its checked declaration (RC5-01) -- plus fixtures/negative/r17-self-declared-demanding-primitive-wraps-a-pin. It is the rule that decides which pins are satisfied, so no pin can be the rule."},{"finding":"RC4-03","why":"A property of the fixture suite's denominator: literal floors in validate_contracts.py compared against the floor declared in each MANIFEST.json, printed in the verdict beside the count. Same class as RC-04, declared unpinnable here already."},{"finding":"RC4-04","why":"A property of a REFUSAL MESSAGE -- that the remedy it advertises is one the checker accepts, and that the one it disclaims is refused. Verified by mutation, not by any registry content: the quantifier refusal states both outcomes in its `accepted` and `not_accepted` fields."},{"finding":"RC4-05","why":"A property of the leak headlines in validate_contracts.py: four kinds, four sentences, and an unknown kind refused rather than folded into another headline. Nothing in a criterion body distinguishes 'not rejected' from 'rejected for the wrong reason'."},{"finding":"RC4-06","why":"A property of tools/run_negative_fixtures.py: a per-fixture exception is that fixture's failure, with its id and file:line, and the suite completes. No conjunct can carry the control flow of the runner."},{"finding":"RC4-07","why":"A property of the VERDICT: the census keys are required on the object about to be printed, with floors under the counts. Pinning the census in this file was the alternative the contract offered and was not taken -- a pin file the checker reads cannot be what proves the checker measured anything."},{"finding":"RC5-01","why":"This finding's repair is the closed literal ADMISSIBLE_LITERAL in validate_contracts.py and the equality check against `#/admissible_ancestors`. Pinning it would be circular in the exact way RC-02's row describes: the allowlist decides which pins are satisfied, so a pin cannot decide the allowlist. The controls are fixtures/negative/r19-allowlist-widened-in-json and its benign twin."},{"finding":"RC5-02","why":"This finding's repair is the widened FINDING_ID, the sweep over the register and the F2 reviews, the unanswered-finding ceiling and the raised floors -- all in validate_contracts.py, and this very row is one of the 28 declarations it forced. A coverage rule cannot be covered by the table it measures. The controls are fixtures/negative/r19-pin-citing-a-review-finding-deleted and its benign twin."},{"finding":"RC5-03","why":"This finding's repair is reading `#/admissible_ancestors_not_admitted`: nothing may be both admitted and not admitted, every name must be a registered primitive, and an operator taking the `quantified` escape must be declared here. The block is now data; a pin over it would be a pin about the pin file. The controls are fixtures/negative/r19-not-admitted-operator-is-also-admitted and its benign twin."},{"finding":"F6X-01","why":"Open residue. The contract is a reference between two registries -- every `exclusive_factory` a record declares must resolve in command-registry.json -- plus a validator check for it. A pin states what a predicate body demands; a dangling factory reference is in no body."},{"finding":"F6X-02","why":"prose contract: 05 section 5 must state the closed `boundary_kind` set and the contested-refs field before contracts can register either. The register's own note is that the enum members would have been invented rather than derived, which is why nothing here registers them yet -- and a pin over an enum this package has not been given would be exactly that invention."}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/unpinnable"},{"id":"pins","why":null,"value":[{"predicate_id":"criterion.DecisionPacket.ready.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G4-08"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-08: the schema omitted `competence_requirement_ref` from `required` while this criterion demanded it, so two implementers derive different packets from one contract -- and the stricter reading destroyed the case DIRECTIVE.md §1.6 most wants preserved, a pure taste decision with no competence prerequisite to name.","require":[{"op":"any","contains_pointers":["/payload/taste_decision","/payload/competence_requirement_ref"],"why":"the packet states its competence footing EXACTLY ONCE, as a disjunction. Collapsing it either way is the finding: demand the ref and taste decisions cannot reach `ready`; demand neither and nothing states the footing."},{"op":"acyclic_record_graph","why":"the dependency graph over the children is acyclic -- a packet that is its own prerequisite is `ready` under any evidence rule"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.Economics.validated.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G4-03"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-03: `external_burden_observations` is required and is an ARRAY, so the protocol's required case -- a profitable venture shifting work onto customers, applicants or contractors -- validated with `[]`. The repair makes the empty case say so explicitly and own it.","require":[{"op":"any","contains_pointers":["/payload/external_burden_observations","/payload/external_burden_account/finding","/payload/external_burden_account/responsible_assignment"],"why":"either burden was observed, or the account states `none_observed` with a reason, a window and a responsible assignment. Deleting the second disjunct restores the silent `[]`; deleting the first makes observation unrecordable."},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/observed_cost","/payload/outstanding_cost"],"why":"the money halves the burden account sits beside"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.Fulfillment.delivered.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G2-05"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"G2-05: delivery must be established by an independently observed result correlated to the delivery adapter's own native object, never by the payment.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/receipts","/payload/delivery_steps"],"why":"the receipts the milestones require, and the milestones themselves"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/performer_assignment","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"a currently accepted performer delivered it"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"AD-013: correlation against the delivery. A settled charge is evidence about money and says nothing about whether the service arrived."}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.Fulfillment.failed.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["RC-01"],"decisions":[],"why":"RC-01 removed `not(native_correlated delivered)` from this criterion because it contradicted the only edge that could reach the phase. A conjunct removed for a stated reason is one removal away from the next one having no reason at all, so what REMAINS is pinned: the failure is owned, not merely declared.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/remaining_duties","/payload/receipts","/payload/service_window"],"why":"the obligations survive on the record, and the receipts actually obtained and the window are recorded -- a failure that recorded none of these would be a closure wearing the word failure"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/performer_assignment","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"CAP-30: failure creates a support/remedy duty, and a duty with no accepted performer is unowned"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.GrievanceCase.remedy_authorized.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G4-02"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-02: `remedy_reservation_refs` was required in the payload and used by 0 predicates, and this criterion carried `nonempty_fields: []` -- so a remedy could be authorized with no reservation at all, which is what made CAP-42's funded remedy prose.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/remedy_reservation_refs"],"why":"an empty reservation list is the exact shape the finding names"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/remedy_reservation_refs","states_exactly":["held","partly_consumed"],"optional":false}],"why":"the funding is ACTUALLY RESERVED: every entry resolves to a live Reservation. A released or expired reservation funds nothing."}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.GrievanceCase.triaged.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G4-01"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-01: `disputed_custodian` is required on GrievanceCase and was read by 0 of 2,255 predicates. The criterion carries the two refs the guard's `neq` compares; erase them here and the comparison one layer up compares absent fields.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/custodian_assignment_ref","/payload/independent_escalation_assignment_ref"],"why":"both sides of the conflict-of-interest comparison must be recorded at triage"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/custodian_assignment_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"the custodian has accepted the case"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G2-01","RC-02"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"The original (d): `performed` demanded byte-identical field_paths to `accepted`, the agreement had no relation to a Fulfillment, and the only native object reachable was the payment -- so a buyer could pay, the Fulfillment stay `proposed`, every Obligation stay `recognized`, and the sale reach `performed` with nothing objecting. AD-013 decides it: delivery, not payment. These three rows ARE that decision, and the recheck's one-hunk weakening removed all three at once.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/fulfillment_ref","/payload/obligation_refs"],"why":"the agreement must NAME its own Fulfillment and its obligations; without these two paths the phases below bind to nothing"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/fulfillment_ref","states_exactly":["delivered"],"optional":false},{"field_path":"/payload/obligation_refs","states_exactly":["discharged","transferred"],"optional":false}],"why":"AD-013: that Fulfillment is currently `delivered` and every linked Obligation is `discharged` or `transferred`. `optional: true` on either would readmit the counterexample with the field simply absent."},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"AD-013: the correlated native object is the DELIVERY. `result: \"performed\"` is what it said before the repair and correlates the sale to the charge."}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.SalesAgreement.terminated_with_residuals.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02: terminating a sale asserted no closure. `terminated_with_residuals` is the state that exists to hold a non-empty due set with an accepted custodian, so a criterion that stopped requiring the duties or the custodian would make the split between the two terminal states decorative.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/obligation_refs","/owner_assignment_ref"],"why":"residuals means surviving duties AND a named owner for them"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/owner_assignment_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"AD-014: the custodian of the residuals has ACCEPTED. A proposed assignment is a nominee, not a custodian."},{"op":"fresh_interval","why":"the residual window is bounded in time; without it a sale sits in residuals indefinitely and the state becomes a parking lot"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.AgentInstance.leased.running.v1","kind":"edge","findings":["AT-M1-04","AS-X-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"The GrantDeliveryReceipt guarantee, pinned where it binds: an executor whose grant did not arrive fails loudly and never completes with less. It is stated on the edge that starts the executor rather than in a guard, because the thirty guard names of section 12.4 are a closed list and this guarantee is not one of them.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/grant_delivery_receipt_ref"],"why":"the receipt the enforcement point wrote is named on the instance before it runs"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/grant_arrival_attested"],"right":true,"why":"and arrival was attested at the first effect. Dropping this is 'the effect proceeds with less', which is section 9's adverse row 7"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.Delegation.offered.accepted.v1","kind":"edge","findings":["AS-M1-01","AX-M2-01"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-D13 and R-X27, pinned where the boundary is crossed: a model-to-model return exists only on a declared procedure edge, delivers TYPED OUTPUTS only, and stores prose at a Ref UNDELIVERED. The typed-member rule extends to every transfer the fixtures name, including the work-order dispatch and the founder-facing brief.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/constraint_set_ref","/payload/return_payload_kind"],"why":"the typed constraint set and the kind of the return are both named on the crossing"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/return_payload_kind"],"right":"typed_outputs","why":"the return delivers typed outputs. This is section 9's adverse row 15: a return delivering prose"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/prose_delivery"],"right":"ref_only","why":"and prose is stored at a Ref rather than delivered -- evidence, never a substitute for a member the receiver can restate as a typed value"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.Fulfillment.delivering.delivered.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-05"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"G2-05/AD-013 at the guard layer: an authenticated attributable delivery event AND native correlation. Each is one line nothing would notice the removal of.","require":[{"op":"transition_basis","event_kind":"record.delivered","why":"delivery needs an authenticated attributable delivery event, and it must be THAT event kind"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"... natively correlated against the delivery adapter, never against the payment"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.Fulfillment.delivering.failed.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["RC-01"],"decisions":[],"why":"The in-flight failure route RC-01 added, and the layer that CAN say what the criterion could not: this edge, unlike the one from `delivered`, asserts that no observation correlates a delivery.","require":[{"op":"transition_basis","event_kind":"record.delivery_failed","why":"an authenticated attributable failure event; without it a delivery can be declared failed by assertion"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","negated":true,"why":"NEGATED on purpose: a failure in flight requires that NO observation correlates a delivery. If this ever appears positively here the route says the opposite of what it exists to say, and a containment check blind to polarity would accept it."}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.GrievanceCase.received.triaged.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G4-01"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-01's counterexample: the implicated custodian named in both fields, `disputed_custodian: true`, and every guard from `received` to `closed` passes. The cure is a guard that READS the flag and COMPARES the two refs.","require":[{"op":"path","pointer":"/payload/disputed_custodian","why":"the required field that 0 of 2,255 predicates read","disjoined":true,"disjoined_why":"G4-01's requirement is conditional by its own terms: the comparison is demanded only where the custodian is the one complained about. The guard reads the flag as the branch test itself -- `any(not(eq(/payload/disputed_custodian, true)), neq(...))` -- so the read IS the disjunction, and demanding it outside one would demand a second read that decides nothing."},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/independent_escalation_assignment_ref","/payload/custodian_assignment_ref"],"why":"where the custodian is the one complained about, the escalation assignment must DIFFER from the custodian assignment -- and the comparison must be between those two refs, not between either of them and something else","disjoined":true,"disjoined_why":"The other branch of the same `any`. On `disputed_custodian: false` there are no two assignments to compare, and a guard that demanded the comparison unconditionally would refuse every undisputed triage -- which is a different requirement from the one G4-01 recorded."}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.ProtectedChange.staged.activated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-07"],"decisions":[],"why":"G2-07: all 173 records carry `owner_component` and no predicate read it, so a component could be removed with its records left unowned.","require":[{"op":"component_custody_transferred","why":"removal of a component must require an accepted successor owner for the records it holds"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.ResponsibilityAssignment.proposed.accepted.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G4-04"],"decisions":[],"why":"G4-04: `custody_basis` and `custody_tie_break_reason` are enums the records carry and nothing compared, so a provisional overlap resolution was indistinguishable from a clean assignment.","require":[{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/custody_basis"],"why":"which branch of the ordered resolver produced this assignment is recorded"},{"op":"eq","right":"provisional_overlap_resolution","contains_pointers":["/payload/custody_basis"],"why":"a recorded overlap must carry custody_basis = provisional_overlap_resolution","disjoined":true,"disjoined_why":"G4-04's enum comparison is demanded only where a contesting mandate exists: `any(not(nonempty(/payload/contesting_mandate_refs)), all(eq(...), neq(...)))`. Demanding `custody_basis = provisional_overlap_resolution` unconditionally would force every clean assignment to claim an overlap it did not have."},{"op":"neq","right":"no_overlap","contains_pointers":["/payload/custody_tie_break_reason"],"why":"... and a tie_break_reason that is not `no_overlap`; without this comparison the enums record a value nothing reads","disjoined":true,"disjoined_why":"The second half of that same conditional branch. `custody_tie_break_reason != no_overlap` is a requirement ABOUT an overlap; with no contesting mandate there is no overlap for it to describe."}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.accepted.performed.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["RC-06"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"RC-06: the three *->performed edges were the only SalesAgreement forward edges with no authenticated-event requirement, and each correlated the sale to `performed` -- its own payment -- which is the reading AD-013 refused one layer below.","require":[{"op":"transition_basis","event_kind":"record.performed","why":"performance needs an authenticated attributable event, as every closure edge already required"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"AD-013 at the guard layer: the correlated native object is the DELIVERY, never the charge"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.accepted.terminated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: terminating a sales agreement is a closure event and must compute the transitive due set.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated","why":"closure requires discharged/not_arisen/transferred for every reachable duty"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.accepted.terminated_with_residuals.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: the residuals state exists to hold a non-empty due set with an accepted custodian for each.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated_with_residuals","why":"the residual branch retains the exact unresolved inventory"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.disputed.performed.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["RC-06"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"RC-06, third of the three *->performed edges -- and the one that matters most, because it is the route out of a dispute.","require":[{"op":"transition_basis","event_kind":"record.performed","why":"performance needs an authenticated attributable event"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"AD-013: correlate to the delivery, not to the payment"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.disputed.terminated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: this is the edge the original counterexample took -- terminate from `disputed` with three `recognized` Obligations and an open SupportCase.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated","why":"the conjunct that refuses that counterexample, by name"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.disputed.terminated_with_residuals.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: this is the route the refused counterexample is told to take instead, so it is the one that must actually hold the duties it inherits.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated_with_residuals","why":"the available route must preserve what the refused route would have dropped"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.partially-performed.performed.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["RC-06"],"decisions":["AD-013"],"why":"RC-06, second of the three *->performed edges.","require":[{"op":"transition_basis","event_kind":"record.performed","why":"performance needs an authenticated attributable event"},{"op":"native_correlated","result":"delivered","why":"AD-013: correlate to the delivery, not to the payment"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.partially-performed.terminated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: terminating a sales agreement is a closure event and must compute the transitive due set.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated","why":"closure requires discharged/not_arisen/transferred for every reachable duty"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.partially-performed.terminated_with_residuals.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: the residuals state exists to hold a non-empty due set with an accepted custodian for each.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated_with_residuals","why":"the residual branch retains the exact unresolved inventory"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.proposed.terminated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: terminating a sales agreement is a closure event and must compute the transitive due set.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated","why":"closure requires discharged/not_arisen/transferred; `due_preserved`'s semantics branch on target_state, so the state named here is the discriminator between the two terminal routes"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.proposed.terminated_with_residuals.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"G2-02/AD-014: the residuals state exists to hold a non-empty due set with an accepted custodian for each.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated_with_residuals","why":"the residual branch retains the exact unresolved inventory; target_state is what selects that reading"}]},{"predicate_id":"edge.SalesAgreement.terminated_with_residuals.terminated.v1","kind":"guard","findings":["G2-02","RC-02"],"decisions":["AD-014"],"why":"The FIFTH closure edge. The recheck found it carrying `due_preserved` and absent from the pinning loop, which covered only the four states that terminate directly -- so the one edge that closes a sale OUT of residuals could lose its due set with every control green. Listing the nine closure edges explicitly rather than generating four is why this one is here.","require":[{"op":"due_preserved","target_state":"terminated","why":"leaving residuals for closure is exactly where the surviving duties must be discharged, not dropped"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present","kind":"guard","findings":["AC-M4-05","AT-M2-03"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"The writer guard on the terminal acceptance edge. Producer-declared completion is not acceptance, and an acceptance with no resolution reason is an interval that timed out wearing a decision's name.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/resolution_reason","/payload/acceptance_owner_ref"],"why":"the reason and the owner who wrote it are both on the record"},{"op":"nonempty","contains_pointers":["/payload/resolution_reason"],"why":"the reason is non-empty as a value, not merely present as a key"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/resolution_reason"],"right":"","why":"and it is not the empty string. Two rows for one requirement because `present`, `nonempty` and `!= \"\"` fail on three different shapes of the same evasion"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.admission.acceptance_role_staffed","kind":"guard","findings":["AC-M3-02","AC-M4-02","AE-M4-05","AT-M2-03"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"R-X09 and R-D09: every acceptance-owner role is a record, and admission in that duty class STOPS while it is unfilled. The mechanism is closed here; who is staffed is the founder's (MD-02).","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/duty_class_id","/payload/acceptance_holder_ref"],"why":"the duty class and the holder that accepts in it are both named on the admission"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/acceptance_holder_ref","states_exactly":["staffed"],"optional":false}],"why":"that holder is CURRENTLY `staffed`. `optional: true` here would readmit the whole finding with the field simply absent, which is what admission-narrowing exists to refuse"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.admission.refusal_names_failed_predicate","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M2-03","AS-M5-01"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"A refusal is a terminal state of the admission record with a named failed predicate and its own acceptance owner. Silence is not refusal. The third row is the FALSE-EXCLUSION control (05 section 9, row 17): the basis of a refusal is a failed predicate, so a genuine job in wording the catalogue does not recognise types as no_match -- a searched answer -- rather than being refused.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/failed_predicate_id","/payload/acceptance_owner_ref"],"why":"a refusal names the predicate that failed AND the party who accepts the refusal"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/failed_predicate_id"],"right":"","why":"and that predicate id is not the empty string, which is how a required field becomes a formality"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/refusal_basis"],"right":"failed_predicate","why":"the BASIS of the refusal is a failed predicate. Unfamiliar wording is not a predicate: this is the row that stops the refusal path swallowing work it merely did not recognise"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.approval.expiry_present","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M2-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"R-X18: every approval carries an expiry, and the monotonic-exposure diff is over effects releasable without a human. Each approval is individually small and the ratchet lives only in their sum, which is the object an executor's own record never shows.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/approved_at","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"an approval and its expiry are both on the record; an approval with no expiry is a permanent one"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/approved_at","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"and the expiry is strictly after the approval, so an expiry set to the approval instant is not an expiry"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.branch.no_model_selected_acceptance_strength","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M3-01"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X19. Procedure admission refuses a branch set in which a model-authored value selects between acceptance strengths for one artifact. Acceptance-strength selection is a guard over journal state and consequence class, never a jump a model's output takes.","require":[{"op":"in","contains_pointers":["/payload/branch_set/selector_kind"],"why":"the selector is one of the two admitted kinds, read off the declared branch set"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/branch_set/selector_kind"],"right":"model","why":"and it is explicitly not a model. Stated twice on purpose: the allow-list is the rule and this is the sentence a reader of the diff sees"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.check.reads_only_non_authorable_fields","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"R-X20. A check interest's precondition reads ONLY fields the producer could not author; a check that reads an authorable field is a check the producer can arm and disarm.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/check_read_field_paths","/payload/non_authorable_field_paths"],"why":"what the check reads and what the producer cannot author are both declared as path lists"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/check_read_field_paths","/payload/non_authorable_field_paths"],"why":"everything the check reads is inside the non-authorable set. Reversing the direction is the finding"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.check.tie_break_strongest_sufficient","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"The other half of R-X20: the tie-break for CHECK interests inverts to strongest sufficient, because the cheapest sufficient check is the one an adversary arms.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/tie_break_rule"],"right":"strongest_sufficient","why":"the tie-break rule is strongest-sufficient, declared as a value rather than left to the resolver's default"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.class.no_total_rank","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M4-02"],"decisions":["AD-015"],"why":"The rule is NEGATIVE -- no total rank and no severity score is computed from the reached set -- and a negative rule needs something positive to check. The derivation declares its ordering as `none` and a guard reads that declaration; a future implementer who reintroduces a rank has to change this literal, which is a line in a diff someone sees.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/class_ordering"],"right":"none","why":"the derivation declares NO ordering over the reached set; any other value is a rank by another name"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"a set, not a list: a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.class.reached_set_gates_all","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M4-02"],"decisions":["AD-015","AD-016"],"why":"AT-M4-02 measured the collapse: an action that is both C2 and C3, gated only by the higher row, loses C2's human requirement. The repair is that the gate evaluator consumes the SET, and these three rows ARE that -- the reached ids and the gated ids are both named, every reached class is inside the gated set, and the two are the same size, so gating a proper subset is not a smaller version of gating the set.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"both sides must be NAMED on the record; without them the two comparisons below bind to nothing"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"every class the derivation reached is in the gated set -- this is 'every reached class's gates apply', stated as a predicate"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"and the two sets are the same size, so a gate set that covers only the highest reached class fails here rather than passing the containment above"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.constraint.standing_interest_writes_proposed_only","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-02"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"The containment that makes layer 3 safe to compute: an interest arms and proposes; it never admits. Its write targets are a subset of the proposed phase, checked rather than described.","require":[{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/write_target_phases"],"right":["proposed"],"why":"every phase this interest may write into is `proposed`. Widening this list is how an interest becomes an admitter"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.criteria.author_not_in_producing_path","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-01"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"The other half of AS-X-01. Freezing criteria the producing path authored freezes the wrong thing, so the author is required to be outside the producing path by membership rather than by intent.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/criteria_author_component_id","/payload/producing_path_component_ids"],"why":"the author and the producing path are both named as ids, which is what makes the membership test mechanical"},{"op":"in","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/criteria_author_component_id","/payload/producing_path_component_ids"],"why":"the author is NOT a member of the producing path. The polarity is the requirement: drop the negation and the guard demands the opposite of what the finding asked for"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.criteria.frozen_before_producing_step","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-01"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"AS-X-01, resolved by R-D11: the capability owner authors the acceptance criteria and they are frozen BEFORE the producing step starts. This closes the fourth unblinded channel into the checker, which no research lane named and one review found.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/criteria_ref","/payload/criteria_frozen_at","/payload/producing_started_at"],"why":"the criteria, the moment they froze and the moment production started are all on the record, so the ordering below is checkable rather than asserted"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/criteria_frozen_at","/payload/producing_started_at"],"why":"frozen STRICTLY BEFORE production started; equality would admit criteria written in the same act as the work they judge"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.custody.admitter_excluded","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"R-X06. The prohibition 'the admitter may not accept work it admitted' yields a mechanism rather than a sentence: the ordered custody resolver SKIPS the admitting identity and continues to the next tier.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/admitting_identity_id","/payload/custody_order_identity_ids"],"why":"the admitting identity and the ordered custody list are both on the record, which is what makes the exclusion checkable"},{"op":"in","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/admitting_identity_id","/payload/custody_order_identity_ids"],"why":"the admitting identity is NOT in the resolved custody order. Polarity is the rule here"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.delegation.width_ceiling_over_distinct_read_sets","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-04"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X16. Fifty sequential consultations, each individually cheap, sit inside one budget and are a fan-out incident. The ceiling is therefore over DISTINCT READ SETS rather than over cost, with the same owner and checkpoint as the depth ceiling.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests","/payload/width_ceiling"],"why":"the read sets seen so far and the ceiling are both on the attempt"},{"op":"lte","contains_pointers":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests","/payload/width_ceiling"],"why":"the COUNT of distinct read sets is at most the ceiling. Replacing this with a cost comparison is the finding"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests"],"why":"and the digests are distinct, so fifty consultations over one read set count once and fifty over fifty count fifty"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.derivation.recompute_matches","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M4-02","AT-M4-02","F6D-03"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"A boundary is real only if what is checked at the moment of the effect is what was granted. `matched` is the phase that asserts the recomputation agreed with the admitted set, so the guard on that edge must compare the two sets and not merely observe that a recomputation happened.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"both sets are named on the record before either is compared"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"nothing the release-time recomputation reached is outside the admitted set"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"and the sets are the same size, so a recomputation that reached MORE cannot take this edge -- it parks, and a park never resolves downward"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids"],"why":"F6D-03, and this is AT-M4-02's harm through the recompute path. reached=[C2,C3] against recomputed=[C3,C3] satisfies the containment and the count equality above, so the record reaches `matched`, the release proceeds, and C2's human requirement on an outward disclosure is gone. `parked_on_mismatch` -- the phase whose row reads \"it never resolves downward\" -- is simply not entered. Duplicate-free on the recomputed side is what makes the count comparison mean set size."},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"and on the admitted side. The sibling guard `guard.class.reached_set_gates_all` uses the identical idiom and is safe only because it shares an edge with `guard.class.no_total_rank`, which asserts `unique` on this field. This guard sits on the NEXT edge, where no guard asserted it at all."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.derivation.writer_is_release_path","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M4-02","AT-M1-04"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"R-L1's writer clause. The derivation record is written only inside the release path and is append-only; an executor's grant scope is a proposal right and C04 performs every release (R-X01).","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/release_path_component_id"],"right":"S1-C04","why":"the release path, named as a component id rather than implied by who happened to write it"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/append_only"],"right":true,"why":"append-only: a derivation that can be rewritten is a boundary that can be moved after the fact"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.identity_allocated_before_release","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M2-06"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"The identity triple is complete and allocated BEFORE release, by C04. A second claimant joins the first and inherits its outcome; without an identity allocated first there is nothing for it to join.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/effect_class_id","/payload/counterparty_id","/payload/payload_digest","/payload/allocated_at"],"why":"the whole identity triple plus the moment of allocation; a partial triple correlates two different effects"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/allocated_at","/payload/claimed_at"],"why":"allocation strictly precedes the claim -- allocated BEFORE release is the ordering, not a field that happens to be filled in"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/allocating_component_id"],"right":"S1-C04","why":"and C04 allocated it, which is the component that performs every release"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.one_per_step","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M3-04"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-L2: one effect per effect step, and the step boundary is the checkpoint, so the step is the idempotency unit. Two effects in one step have one journal position between them and no way to resume between them.","require":[{"op":"lte","contains_pointers":["/payload/effect_refs"],"right":"1","why":"at most ONE effect reference per step"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/idempotency_unit"],"right":"step","why":"and the declared idempotency unit is the step, so a retry replays the same boundary"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.holder.no_veto","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M4-01","AT-M3-03"],"decisions":["AD-022"],"why":"AE-M4-01: four protected holders that could each refuse a shed can hold an empty bucket hostage with no override anywhere. A holder may negotiate and never veto, and its pre-declared minimum is EVIDENCE rather than consent.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/veto_right"],"right":false,"why":"the holder carries no veto right. Flipping this to true is the finding, restored"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/stated_minimum_status"],"right":"evidence","why":"and its stated minimum is recorded AS evidence, so a shed records it and proceeds rather than waiting on it"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.holder.not_retired_while_duty_live","kind":"guard","findings":["AC-M1-05","AC-M4-05"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"Retirement is refused while any duty the holder carries is live, and the successor that inherits the address and the residual duties is named. A closure that completed while a grievance route was unowned is a broken promise recorded as a clean end.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/successor_holder_ref"],"why":"the holder this one retires INTO is named; retiring into nothing is the unowned route"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/live_duty_refs"],"right":[],"why":"and no duty it holds is still live"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.intake.per_origin_subcap_preserves_intake","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M2-01","AS-M4-05"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"AS-M2-01: an adversary filing many well-formed complaints through the published route exhausts the cap, and the next write -- from a legitimate complainant -- is refused at exactly the object whose purpose is to guarantee an outside party can always reach someone. R-X17: the cap overflows to a named destination with an alarm and a reader; it never refuses intake.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/origin_subcap","/payload/counterparty_subcap","/payload/overflow_destination_ref","/payload/overflow_alarm_reader_ref"],"why":"both sub-caps, the overflow destination and the READER of its alarm. A destination nobody reads is a queue nobody polls, which this chapter names as a silent-drop shape"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/intake_refused"],"right":false,"why":"and intake was not refused. This is the row that distinguishes R-X17's repair from the design it repairs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.interest.precondition_invokes_no_model","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-01","AS-M5-03"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"R-L3's hardest line: NO precondition may invoke a model. Arming is a computed, unstoppable set evaluated by deterministic code over typed fields after every committed transition, and a model in a precondition makes the set neither computed nor unstoppable.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/precondition_evaluator_kind"],"right":"deterministic","why":"the evaluator is deterministic, declared as a value a guard can read"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/precondition_evaluator_kind"],"right":"model","why":"and it is explicitly not a model -- the prohibition stated as its own conjunct, because an allow-list widened by one entry is a quieter change than a deleted refusal"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.interest.writer_keys_not_self_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-02"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"AS-M5-02: a trial interest declaring a writer key over an existing sole-writer kind takes custody of a kind by asserting it. An interest declares no writer key of its own, and writer-key authority comes from outside it.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/self_declared_writer_keys"],"right":[],"why":"the self-declared writer key list is EMPTY. `[]` rather than absent: an absent field is a field nobody looked at"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/writer_key_authority_kind"],"right":"external","why":"and the authority for any writer key it uses is external to the interest"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.launcher.stripping_positive_control","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"AS-X-02 names the asymmetry: three candidates gave grant DELIVERY a positive control and none gave grant REMOVAL one. This is that control -- a probe skill that declares a tool grant, whose ARRIVAL at the enforcement point is the failure. A stripper that has never been observed to strip is a stripper nobody has seen work.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_ref","/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"why":"the probe and its result are both recorded; a control with no recorded reading is a control nobody ran"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"right":"absent_at_enforcement_point","why":"the probe's declared grant was ABSENT at the enforcement point. This literal is the whole positive control"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"right":"arrived_at_enforcement_point","why":"and it did not arrive. Stated as its own conjunct because the failing reading is the one a reader must be able to find by name"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.readset.delivered_equals_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M3-01","AX-M4-01","F6D-02"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"AD-017: a step declares its read set and the loader delivers and RECORDS it. That converts 'the checker did not read the producer's rationale' from an assertion into a manifest fact. The equality is checked against the loader's own record, never against the caller's claimed read list.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"the loader's record carries BOTH what was asked for and what it actually delivered"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"nothing declared went undelivered -- the half that catches a starved step"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"and nothing UNDECLARED was delivered -- the half that catches the producer's rationale field arriving in a checker's inputs, which is section 9's adverse row 2"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set"],"why":"F6D-02. A READ SET IS A SET. `subset` is element-wise and `count` counts duplicates, so declared=[\"/artifact\",\"/artifact\"] against delivered=[\"/artifact\",\"/producer_rationale\"] satisfies both rows above -- the producer's rationale reaches the checker while the loader's own record certifies that it did not. Duplicate-free on both sides, plus the containment and the count equality, IS set equality; this is the half that closes it on the declared side."},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"and on the delivered side, for the same reason in the other direction. Pin 34 on `guard.class.no_total_rank` had already written the sentence -- \"a set, not a list: a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled\" -- and the idiom was not swept: six guards use `subset`, two pair it with a count to mean set equality, and neither of those two carried `unique` until now."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.reservation.lapse_independent_of_consent","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M3-01","AE-M4-01","AE-M4-03","F6C-03"],"decisions":["AD-022"],"why":"R-X24 and AD-022: a reserve under a non-rolling allowance is waste rather than thrift, so reserved-but-unconsumed allowance lapses at the declared point INDEPENDENTLY of the holder's consent, and the quantity that lapsed is recorded per lane per window.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/lapse_point"],"why":"the lapse point is declared on the reservation rather than decided under pressure"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/lapse_requires_holder_consent"],"right":false,"why":"the lapse does NOT require the holder's consent. This is the whole of AD-022's second half"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/lapsed_unconsumed_quantity"],"why":"and the quantity that lapsed unconsumed is recorded -- an unrecorded lapse is the waste nobody can price"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/lapse_occurrence_ref"],"why":"F6C-03: the reservation names the ScheduleOccurrence that IS its lapse. The adverse case as worded is a reservation that takes no transition at all -- nothing fires when the point passes -- so the lapse has to be an event rather than a field nobody reads"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/lapse_occurrence_ref","states_exactly":["due","admitted","running","completed","missed","disposed"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that occurrence has left `planned`, so the durable timer sweep has actually reached it. `planned` is excluded on purpose: a scheduled lapse nobody woke for is the case this row exists to refuse"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.skill.declared_grant_inert","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"R-X15. A skill's declared tool grant is INERT: the launcher computes the tool set from the work order's grant and strips tool-granting frontmatter. Content-hash pinning is re-checked at USE rather than at install, because a stale borrowed skill passes shape checks.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_tool_grant_effect"],"right":"inert","why":"the skill's own declared grant grants nothing"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/tool_set_source"],"right":"work_order","why":"and the tool set comes from the work order, which is the only place a grant is authorised"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/content_hash_rechecked_at_use"],"right":true,"why":"the content hash is re-checked at use; a hash checked only at install pins a file that has since moved"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.supersession.walks_derived_closure","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M1-02","F6C-15"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-G06. Superseding an entry walks the transitive closure of derived entries naming the superseded value and re-decides or flags each reopened acceptance. The `present` rows rather than `nonempty` rows are deliberate: a supersession with an EMPTY closure must reopen nothing and must pass, which is section 9's benign row 11.","require":[{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"the closure was computed -- present even when empty, because an absent closure and an empty one are different facts"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs"],"why":"and what it reopened was recorded, likewise present even when empty"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/closure_walk_completed"],"right":true,"why":"the walk ran to completion; a partial walk reports an empty closure that looks exactly like a real one"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs","/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"every acceptance reopened came from the closure, so the reopen set cannot be authored beside the walk"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/accepted_closure_member_refs","/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs"],"why":"F6C-15, AND IT IS THE DIRECTION THE ADVERSE CASE NEEDS. The four rows above contain the reopen set within the closure -- a real guarantee, and a DIFFERENT one: with derived_closure_refs = [an accepted outcome] and reopened_acceptance_refs = [], subset([], [x]) holds and every one of them passes the case 05 section 9 row 11 says must fail. Every accepted outcome in the closure is in the reopen set."},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/accepted_closure_member_refs","/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"and the accepted members are themselves drawn from the closure, so the new field cannot be emptied beside a walk that found something. The benign row is untouched: an EMPTY closure has no accepted members, reopens nothing, and passes."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.trust.derived_is_floor_of_inputs","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M2-04","AX-M3-01"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X13. Per-item trust level is a required manifest field, and a derived item's level is the FLOOR of its inputs': deriving from an untrusted entry cannot launder it into a trusted one.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/derived_trust_level","/payload/minimum_input_trust_level"],"why":"per-item levels are a manifest field, not a per-record one, and both sides of the floor are recorded"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/derived_trust_level","/payload/minimum_input_trust_level"],"why":"the derived level IS the minimum input level; anything else is a promotion, which the guard below governs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.trust.promotion_is_an_effect_with_a_non_model_discriminator","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-01"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"AS-M5-01: an untrusted entry promoted to attested by the investigating activation is the activation reading its own output back as corroboration. Promotion is an effect with a discriminator that is not a model, and the corroborant is out of band.","require":[{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/trust_promotion_discriminator_kind"],"right":"model","why":"the discriminator is not a model. This is the row the finding is about"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/trust_promotion_rule"],"right":"out_of_band_corroborant","why":"and the rule names the out-of-band corroborant, so a promotion sourced from the investigating path has no route through this guard"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.grant.declared_resolves_to_live_grant","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-06"],"decisions":["R-L2"],"why":"A declared grant is RESOLVED at admission, not recorded. The two rows are the two halves of that: the list is on the record, and every ref in it currently stands at `active` in the Grant registry -- which IS the grant policy, because a second grant-policy record would be a second authority over one question. Arrival is a different fact from scope: `grant_arrival_attested` on the lease edge attests that a grant arrived, never that it was the right one, and this is the half that checks scope.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/declared_grants"],"why":"the declaration is present and not empty; an empty list would satisfy any resolution rule vacuously, which is how the field read as enforcement while nothing read it"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/declared_grants","states_exactly":["active"],"optional":false}],"why":"and every ref in it resolves to a Grant standing at `active`. `optional: false` is the load-bearing half -- with it optional, a declaration that resolves to nothing passes, which is the state the field was in."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.protected.control_object_change_authorized","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-07"],"decisions":[],"why":"The guard carries the PHASE half of the rule and the schema carries the PRESENCE half, and they are stated in different places on purpose. Phase: any protected change this subject names stands at `authorized`, `staged`, `activated` or `verified` -- `proposed` and `reviewed` are excluded because reviewed means an assessment completed and authorized means a competent decision was taken, and it is the decision the rule is about. `rejected`, `rollback_required` and `rolled_back` are excluded for the obvious reason. Presence: `protected_change_ref` is `required` on ConsequenceClassDefinition, FieldAuthority and StandingHolder, whose listed transitions are always control-object changes, and on ConfigurationVersion it is required by `dependentRequired` exactly when `control_object_kind` is declared -- which is what keeps the paired benign case, an ordinary configuration admitted normally, from being refused. The binding is therefore `optional: true` HERE and must not be read as an escape: it is the one place where a record may legitimately name no protected change, and PS-CONTROL-OBJECT-CONDITIONAL in validate_contracts.py is what stops that place widening.","require":[{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/protected_change_ref","states_exactly":["authorized","staged","activated","verified"],"optional":true}],"why":"a named protected change stands at authorized or later. Detached or weakened to `proposed`, a floor changes on the strength of a proposal nobody decided."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.second_claimant_inherits","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-11","F6C-08"],"decisions":[],"why":"The join, stated as a predicate. The triple is named on the record -- it is the natural key declared in `EffectIdentity.identity`, so two allocations of one triple are one allocation -- and the claim names the operation intent whose outcome it inherits, which must already stand at `released` or `observed`. `optional: false` is what makes the second row bind: with it optional, a claim naming nothing passes and the inheritance is a sentence again. What this repair does NOT add is an exclusive-factory command: the nine records that declare one name a command in a registry with its own generated schema, and three of those nine name a command the command registry does not contain at all -- a pre-existing gap, recorded here rather than widened.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/effect_class_id","/payload/counterparty_id","/payload/payload_digest","/payload/first_claimant_ref"],"why":"the business triple and the first claimant are all named on the record before the claim is judged; the triple is the declared natural key and the claimant is what the inheritance points at"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/first_claimant_ref","states_exactly":["released","observed"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the first claimant already released or was observed, so there is an outcome to inherit. A claim cannot invent one, which is the difference between joining and re-releasing."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.manifest.omission_and_class_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-01"],"decisions":["R-D14"],"why":"06 section 2 states three requirements and this pin is all three. `present` rather than `nonempty` on `dropped_constraints` is deliberate and is the same choice pin 51 makes for a closure walk: a manifest that dropped nothing must record that it dropped nothing, and an absent list and an empty one are different facts. `assembled_for_class` is `nonempty` because a manifest is always assembled FOR something, and `unique` because it is a class SET -- pin 34's sentence, a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled. The count equality is the per-item join the chapter calls the load-bearing part: `item_trust_levels` was `array<string>` with no length join to `captured_inputs`, so a manifest could carry one trust level for twelve delivered items and satisfy every check.","require":[{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/dropped_constraints"],"why":"the dropped-constraint list was computed -- present even when empty, because nothing dropped is a result and an absent list is a silence"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/assembled_for_class","/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/captured_inputs"],"why":"the class set the manifest was assembled for is named, and the two lists the join below compares are both on the record"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/assembled_for_class"],"why":"the assembled-for set is a SET; a repeated class id makes a reuse check count wrong in the direction that admits the reuse"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/captured_inputs"],"why":"one trust level per delivered item. 06 section 2 says the per-item part is the load-bearing part, and without this the field is an unindexed list of strings beside an unrelated list of inputs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.constraint.set_bound_at_transfer","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-02"],"decisions":["R-X27"],"why":"A transfer names its constraint set and that set currently stands at `issued`. The phase row matters as much as the presence row: `ConstraintSet` is single-phase, so `issued` is the only state a real one can be in, and a binding that accepted any phase would accept a ref to a record that was never issued at all.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/constraint_set_ref"],"why":"the transfer names a constraint set rather than narrating its constraints in the body it is carrying"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/constraint_set_ref","states_exactly":["issued"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that set resolves and stands at `issued`. `optional: false`: a transfer with no constraint set is the untyped boundary this rule exists to close"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.calibration.current_for_checker","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-03"],"decisions":["AM-01"],"why":"06 section 7: a verdict from an instrument whose refusal rate on correct artifacts is unrecorded is `unresolved`, and it MAY NOT SATISFY AN ACCEPTANCE GATE. The calibration record itself is well built and is credited -- criterion.InstrumentCalibration.run.v1 requires a planted-defect detection rate AND a clean-case refusal rate AND session observations AND a protected change, so a calibration cannot reach `run` on a detection rate alone. What was missing was anything joining that record to a verdict. `run` and only `run`: `scheduled` has no refusal rate yet and `superseded` is not current. Second half (F6B-03, lane r8-contracts-i): the join is now to the calibration OF THIS CHECKER. Before it an acceptance could name any instrument current calibration, and r18-acceptance-on-an-uncalibrated-checker does not reach that -- it is about a calibration being absent, not about it belonging to someone else.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/calibration_ref","/payload/checker_id"],"why":"the acceptance names the calibration of the instrument that produced its verdict, and names that instrument: an interval with no checker_id is unresolved, never pass"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/calibration_ref","states_exactly":["run"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that calibration stands at `run`. This is the conjunct that makes 'unresolved, never pass' a refusal rather than a sentence"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/checker_id"],"why":"and the calibration it names belongs to that checker. This row is the BACKSTOP and not the control: it can say the conjunct mentions checker_id and cannot say which record each side of the equality reads, which is the F6Y-02 lesson stated against a check written in its shadow. The binding control is the structural comparison in validate_contracts.py, and the R45 adverse half is the counterexample that leaves this row satisfied and is refused anyway."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.obligation.production_mode_named","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-01"],"decisions":["R-G01"],"why":"The field half of G-01. The guard demands the two facts the payload conditional needs to bind: the mode is named, and whether this duty's class can exhaust is RECORDED rather than inferred from the class id by whoever reads it next. The refusal of `production_mode == none` on an exhaustible class is the conditional on the payload itself (PS-EXHAUSTIBLE-DUTY-CONDITIONAL), for the same reason the control-object rule is split: a rule that binds on a VALUE of one field is a schema conditional, and writing it as a disjunction in the guard would need two rows excused from demanding their own conjunct.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/production_mode"],"why":"the production mode is named on the obligation. `none` is a NAMED mode and is admissible for a duty class that cannot exhaust; what is refused is silence, and then `none` on a class that can"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/duty_class_can_exhaust"],"why":"and the exhaustibility of the duty class is on the record. `present` rather than `nonempty` because it is a boolean and `false` is an answer"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.launch.actual_compared_to_expected","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-04","F6C-14"],"decisions":["R-X12"],"why":"Both counts are on the closing run and the definition they came from is an admitted procedure version, so the divergence is readable from the record. The divergence is NOT stored as a third number: a stored difference can disagree with the two values it is derived from, and this package has paid for that class of mistake more than once. A procedure that checkpoints at every step boundary makes more launches than one that does not, and before these two fields nothing in the package would have noticed.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/actual_native_launch_count","/payload/expected_native_launch_count","/payload/workflow_ref"],"why":"the actual count, the expected count and the procedure version are all named at close. `actual_native_launch_count` is optional at registration for the obvious reason and is demanded HERE, which is what \"required at close\" means"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/workflow_ref","states_exactly":["admitted","restricted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the expected count came from a procedure version that was actually admitted, rather than from a draft someone edited after the run started"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-09"],"decisions":["R-X10"],"why":"The guard demands the two facts the payload conditional turns on; the conditional itself refuses `no_progress` with no recipient, because the rule binds on a VALUE of `error_class` and that is a schema conditional rather than a guard row. `present` on `no_progress_terminal` rather than `nonempty`: it is a boolean and `false` is an answer. Note what is still true and is not this pin's business -- `no_progress` is not a lifecycle PHASE, so `terminal` means the record reached `blocked` carrying that class, which is what the attachment says.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/error_class"],"why":"the error class is named on a record that is about to stop moving"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/no_progress_terminal"],"why":"and whether that class is terminal is recorded rather than inferred from the phase by whoever reads it next"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.ExhaustionDecision.written.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-02"],"decisions":["R-G01"],"why":"F6C-02: `07` section 6 and `05` section 7 both require that the breach-or-perform decision leaves a record naming which was chosen and why, and NO RECORD TYPE WAS IT. `ShedDecision` is written when C02 proposes and C01 authorises a SHED; its `minimum_breached` is a fact about a shed lane under pressure, not about an empty bucket meeting a due obligation, and one record carrying both would collapse the distinction the exhaustion rule exists to draw. This pin holds the new record's only criterion to the six things the chapter says it carries. The criterion body is authored by tools/phase_content.py and this table is not, which is the point: weakening the derivation and regenerating cannot satisfy these rows.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/obligation_ref","/payload/window","/payload/branch","/payload/route_ref","/payload/alerted_recipient_ref","/payload/reason","/payload/proposing_component_id","/payload/authorising_component_id"],"why":"the branch, the route, the alerted recipient, the due obligation, the window and the reason are all on the record, with the proposing and authorising components named. Neither branch is silent and neither branch is automatic -- a record that reached `written` naming no branch is the sentence this record was created to stop being"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/route_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the route is an assignment that is currently `accepted`. A route nobody holds is not a route, which is the same failure the `production_mode: none` refusal catches one record earlier"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.ExistenceJustification.admitted.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6D-12"],"decisions":["R-L4"],"why":"F6D-12: the load-bearing transition really is mechanical -- `test_can_return_false == true` is checked, which is what `05` section 4 says makes the existence gate mechanical rather than rhetorical -- and the one typed field the six-reason table needs was missing, so a seventh reason was admissible on the record built to refuse it. This pin holds the two halves the derivation must keep: the test that can fail, and the reason paired with its unit. The criterion body is authored by tools/phase_content.py and this table is not, which is the point: weakening the derivation and regenerating cannot satisfy these rows.","require":[{"op":"any","contains_pointers":["/payload/reason_kind","/payload/reason_unit"],"why":"the reason and the unit it predicates on are decided TOGETHER, as a disjunction over the six pairs `05` section 4 tabulates. Collapsing it to two independent enums admits a reason applied to another reason's unit, which returns undecidable -- and undecidable reads as satisfied"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/decidable_test","/payload/admitting_mandate_ref","/payload/attributable_identity_ref","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"and the three structural conjuncts the review credited stay: a decidable test is recorded, the admitting mandate is named and the approval carries an expiry. A justification naming a capability is uncheckable"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.UnmatchedPoolEntry.landed.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-11"],"decisions":[],"why":"F6C-11: the one case in the amendment where a rule's own words state a comparison and the criterion carrying it makes none. 33 of the 46 criteria on the fifteen new records contain no comparison operator and that is disclosed rather than hidden -- the substantive comparisons live in the 30 guards. This one is different because `05` section 6 states the comparison in the sentence the criterion exists to carry. Pinned so that regenerating a weakened derivation cannot quietly return it to collecting both operands and comparing neither.","require":[{"op":"lt","why":"retention STRICTLY EXCEEDS the longest plausible outage. `lte` would admit an entry whose retention ends exactly at the outage horizon, which is the boundary the rule is about -- and `exceeds` is the chapter's own word"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/work_record_ref","/payload/retention_until","/payload/longest_plausible_outage","/payload/retention_span","/payload/landed_at","/payload/alarm_reader_ref","/payload/alarm_raised_at","/payload/residue_note"],"why":"and the operands stay REQUIRED. Requiring `longest_plausible_outage` as a per-entry field is the right answer to `stated, not assumed` and is more than the prose promised; the comparison is what it was missing, not the collection"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/retention_span","/payload/retention_until","/payload/landed_at"],"why":"and the span the ceiling is applied to is the offset between the two instants, not a third number the producer states beside them. This row is the SHAPE half only: it says the equality mentions all three pointers, and a pointer set cannot say which side of the subtraction each one is on. The half that can is the structural read in validate_contracts.py under THE RETENTION SPAN IS NOT TIED TO THE INSTANTS IT MEASURES, and the two are deliberately not the same author (F6C-11, the remainder)"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.FailureRecord.retry_admitted.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-10"],"decisions":["R-X10"],"why":"F6C-10: `attempt_ceiling` was on `FailureRecord` alone, optional, and in zero predicates, so a retry was admitted without anything comparing the attempts already made against the bound that exists to stop them. The generic `retry_admitted` phase spec is shared by many records and was NOT edited -- this criterion comes from a FailureRecord override, and this pin is what stops that override being quietly returned to the generic one.","require":[{"op":"lt","why":"the attempts already made are STRICTLY FEWER than the ceiling. `lte` would admit the attempt that reaches it, which is the one the ceiling exists to refuse"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/work_order_ref","/payload/attempt_refs","/payload/attempt_ceiling","/payload/next_discriminator"],"why":"and the ceiling, the attempts and the new discriminator are all named on the record. A retry with no discriminator repeats an unchanged failure, which is the record's own invariant"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.workorder.critical_fields_authority_excludes_the_resuming_attempt","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-13"],"decisions":["R-D18"],"why":"F6C-13: `critical_fields` carried its authorship rule in prose (`05` section 3 R-D18) and in ZERO predicates -- optional on WorkflowDefinition and StepExecution, read by nothing. Pinned on the ADMISSION edge because R-D18 fixes the authority AT PROCEDURE ADMISSION; a guard that is not demanded on that edge lets the marking be written later, by the attempt that benefits from it. The pin is over the GUARD BODY; that the admission edge DEMANDS it is the second half, pinned in `pinned_attachments` -- F6D-01 measured thirty detachments undetected by conjunct pins alone.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/critical_fields","/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref"],"why":"a work order names the fields whose change forces re-decision AND the authority that marked them. AT-M1-02 asked for the marking to be recorded on the WorkOrder or the FieldAuthority and it was on neither, so the rule was prose only"},{"op":"eq","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref","/owner_assignment_ref"],"why":"and that authority is NOT the order's own owner_assignment_ref. The envelope owner is the party resuming the work; R-D18's whole content is that the marking authority is never the resuming attempt, and equality here is that failure"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and it resolves to an assignment actually in `accepted` at admission. A ref to a proposed or expired assignment names a marking authority that does not hold"}]}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/pins"},{"id":"pinned_transitions","why":"A conjunct pin holds a guard to what it must DEMAND. It says nothing about whether the edge exists at all, and RC-01 was exactly that shape of defect: every guard was well formed, every criterion was sourced, and a delivery that failed in flight had nowhere to go. Deleting a transition is a one-line edit to record-registry.json that no check in this package noticed -- phase reachability is not checked, and a phase with fewer ways in is not a phase with fewer conjuncts. These rows are hand-written for the same reason the conjunct pins are: nothing derives them, so regenerating cannot satisfy them.\n\nF6D-04 extends this table from 6 rows to 32. The six it had were all on `Fulfillment` and `SalesAgreement`, and NOT ONE of the fifteen WORK-1.1 records had a pinned transition -- so the earlier repair pinned the instances it found and the new layer arrived with none. Measured: three edge deletions, `ConsequenceDerivation: recomputed_at_release -> parked_on_mismatch`, `AcceptanceInterval: in_acceptance -> rejected` and `StandingHolder: staffed -> suspended`, three validator runs, three exit 0. Every edge named in `pinned_attachments` is pinned here as well, because the two attacks are different: deleting the CALL leaves the route and removes the check, deleting the TRANSITION leaves the check and removes the route, and each control is blind to the other's attack.","value":[{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivering","to":"failed","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"A delivery that fails IN FLIGHT. Without this edge `delivering` exits only to `delivered` or `superseded`, so the only route to `failed` runs through a delivery that was correlated -- which is the contradiction RC-01 names."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"failed","to":"refunded","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"Refunding an undelivered sale -- the commonest refund there is -- was unrepresentable while `refunded` had one inbound edge, from `delivered`."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivered","to":"refunded","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"The other half of the same requirement: `refunded` is reachable from BOTH, and a repair that added one route by deleting the other would satisfy a looser reading of the finding."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"validated","to":"delivering","findings":["RC-08"],"why":"RC-08: without it the generic validation branch and the delivery branch are disjoint, so a validated Fulfillment can only be delivered by a route that skipped validation."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivering","to":"delivered","findings":["G2-05"],"why":"The edge AD-013's delivery evidence hangs on. Its conjuncts are pinned above; this pins that there is an edge for them to be conjuncts OF."},{"record":"SalesAgreement","from":"terminated_with_residuals","to":"terminated","findings":["G2-02"],"why":"The route OUT of residuals. Without it `terminated_with_residuals` is a terminal state that keeps its duties forever, which is a different contract from the one AD-014 decided."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"declared_done","to":"in_acceptance","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.criteria.author_not_in_producing_path`, `guard.criteria.frozen_before_producing_step` are attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"in_acceptance","to":"accepted","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"in_acceptance","to":"rejected","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AdmissionRecord","from":"opened","to":"admitted","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.admission.acceptance_role_staffed`, `guard.custody.admitter_excluded` are attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AdmissionRecord","from":"opened","to":"parked","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.intake.per_origin