nd the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.Deployment.effect_unknown.v1","phases":["unknown_effect"],"why":"The sentence names `unknown_effect`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while Deployment spells the same phase `effect_unknown`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.EvaluationPlan.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while EvaluationPlan spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.EvaluatorProfile.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while EvaluatorProfile spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_p